## TASK
Deliver the request end to end inside {{root}}, as the roles of the spine would: product, design, acceptance, craft, review. Work through the tool: start with `next` and follow it; every step says what it is for, which fields it needs and which skills to read BEFORE it; a refusal says WHAT, WHY and HOW. The tool runs your RED/GREEN/probe commands itself: never claim a result, record the command.
- Order: map every request sentence to promises and concrete scenarios (GIVEN data / WHEN input / THEN result) BEFORE reading code; then look for CONFLICTS between each sentence and what the system already does (read code only for that); a conflict is a question for the owner, or, when absent, your recorded decision. Then tests from the public interface, then reuse and design, then code.
- Tests: only through the public port (CLI, HTTP, public API), in the user's words, no process ids, no production symbols. For each test note the nearest plausible WRONG behaviour it would still accept (`still passes if`) and tighten it when that behaviour violates a promise. Every design law becomes a generated property. No test depends on the wall clock to decide its outcome or on the order of a set: when the product stamps whole seconds, assert relations between stamps, never exact times and never a sleep longer than the stamp resolution needs. Unit up to 10 ms, integration seconds: a slow test is a defect, fix it now.
- A concurrency, crash or kill test must PROVE ITS PREMISE from the outside: observe that the overlap or interruption really happened through what the public port and the files the product writes show (a second writer is seen waiting on the lock; the process was stopped while its temporary file existed) and assert the exact before-or-after state; a test that would pass whether or not the premise occurred proves nothing. A test hook inside production code is a design decision: record it and its reason, never a default. If the premise cannot be reached from outside, say so in the documents as an unproven limit instead of testing a weaker thing under the stronger name. Use pytest's default tmp location for tests.
- Test cost is measured, not guessed: run the suite once with `--durations=10` (when the authority text forbids the whole suite, the test directories it names), then run only the test files you added nine times in a row and record their median and slowest time as the `test_cost` receipt (they must pass nine times: a test that fails once in nine is flaky, fix it). Keep every unit test under 10 ms and every integration test within seconds (few cards, short timeouts; one process test per behaviour, not per scenario). A sleep used only to make two timestamps differ is a defect: pick a design where the order is observable without waiting, or list the test over budget with its reason.
- Seen RED first: a check you have not seen fail is not evidence. A test revised after the implementation exists is shown failing against the tree as it was before the feature (an archived copy of the previous commit under the workspace), and that run is recorded.
- Code: implement through the public port to GREEN. If the code you touch breaks the type rule (primitives only in adapters; domain takes value objects) prefactor FIRST in a separate commit, behaviour unchanged, no test edited. Refactor L1-L4 in separate green commits before the behaviour edit. Decisions live in rich owners (value object, entity, aggregate), not in services or scripts. Paradigm and file layout are those named in the authority text; when it names none, mirror the sibling code (never impose a style the repository does not use); no duplication; one source of truth.
- Behaviour you were not asked to change stays: do not break or widen existing public behaviour (reserved names, existing statuses, existing ordering, existing commands) without a recorded owner decision. The request's own sentences outrank any compatibility heuristic: when keeping an old name valid would make the requested command unavailable in some case, that is a conflict for the owner, not a silent choice.
- A command you add beside an existing sibling does each cross-cutting thing the way the sibling does (when it reads the clock relative to taking the lock, how it saves, how it reports errors, what it stamps); read the sibling first and treat every difference as a recorded decision.
- Every option a command shows in `--help` must work, and every option that works is shown: check help against behaviour for each command you add. The command you add changes nothing (empty source, same state) writes nothing and bumps no revision; do not alter that behaviour of other commands. A new reserved word or verb never makes a name that was valid before invalid: check against names the system already accepts.
- State, protocol, concurrency, crash: decide explicitly whether a formal model pays (Quint); if you skip it, say why in one line.
- Review of yourself: the step `selfreview` asks 17 dimensions; you may not leave debt: fix, then record `fixed:` with evidence, or justify `n/a:`. An independent reviewer and a blind examiner will check you afterwards.
- Documents (design record, DECISIONS, summary) are generated by `brief --out {{root}}/docs/<short-feature-name>` and committed with the change: the reviewer reads only the repository, so the formal-model decision, the conflict decisions and the test-cost receipt must be there. Never hand-write what the tool can derive; update what your change made false; generate them again after the last record.
- Documents say exactly what a test proves and what it does not (a kill while a lock is held is not a kill during the write; a sample of runs is not a proof). Never write a guarantee your tests do not establish; one place states each number.
- Skills are directories with a SKILL.md; the tool prints the exact paths before each step; a summary in the system prompt is not the skill. Read what the step lists, apply it, list the paths you read. When a later finding makes an earlier step's record stale, record that step again (the latest revision wins) instead of leaving a false claim.

